-- url     : https://prove2.me/theorems/715dac2a-5778-4ef4-98c7-915efa8706b2
-- title:
--   Normalized source data is preserved below its allocation bound
-- statement:
--   Let $g>0$, let three original integer lists have lengths at most $n$, and let $A,B,C$ be their strictly increasing normalized lists. Suppose memory $\mu$ satisfies the normalized-data specification, including their representations, band lookup tables, owner records, and the three incidence arrays with returned count $r$. For each list $X$, let $v_X,e_X$ be its value and endpoint bases; let $t,\rho,v$ be the table, record, and incidence bases. Put $R=3\operatorname{bands}(n,g)^2$. Suppose $\nu$ agrees with $\mu$ below $b$ and
--
--   $$v_X+n\le b,\quad e_X+2+2n\le b\quad(X=A,B,C),$$
--
--   $$t+R\le b,\quad\rho+4R\le b,\quad v+3gR\le b.$$
--
--   Then the same normalized-data specification holds in $\nu$, with all lists and $r$ unchanged. This permits later allocations without losing the original finite-source witnesses.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/SourceFiniteRetention.lean#L9-L53

